---
import Exhibit from "../components/Exhibit.astro";
import TrafficLight from "../components/TrafficLight.astro";
import ChapterOpener from "../components/ChapterOpener.astro";
import Term from "../components/Term.astro";
import PractitionerNotes from "../components/PractitionerNotes.astro";
import Definitions from "../components/Definitions.astro";
import Pager from "../components/Pager.astro";
import { load, pct, type Frame } from "../lib/data";

const metrics = load("metrics");
const baselF = load<Frame>("basel");
const basel = metrics.basel;
const long = metrics.var_long;
const rows = [
  { key: "gaussian", name: "Normal distribution (RiskMetrics)" },
  { key: "hist", name: "Historical simulation" },
  { key: "garch", name: "GARCH(1,1)" },
  { key: "gjr", name: "GJR-GARCH" },
  { key: "fhs", name: "Filtered historical simulation" },
  { key: "split", name: "Conformal, split" },
  { key: "dtaci", name: "Conformal, DtACI" },
  { key: "aci", name: "Conformal, ACI", focus: true },
];
const days: number = basel.aci.days;
const label = `Basel traffic light by day. Normal distribution: green on ${pct(basel.gaussian.green)} of days; conformal ACI: ${pct(basel.aci.green)}.`;
const longRows = ["gaussian", "hist", "garch", "fhs", "split", "aci"];
const longNames: Record<string, string> = Object.fromEntries(rows.map((r) => [r.key, r.name]));
const n10 = long.aci.n_nonoverlap;
---
<section class="chapter" id="regulator" aria-labelledby="regulator-h">
  <ChapterOpener n="7" title="The regulator's view" minutes={3} question="Would a bank supervisor accept this model?" hid="regulator-h" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Bank supervisors score VaR models with the Basel <Term id="traffic-light" title="Basel's backtest: count breaches of 99% VaR over the last 250 days. 0–4 green, 5–9 yellow, 10 or more red.">traffic light</Term>.
      Every day they count the breaches of the 99% limit over the past 250 trading days. Up to four is
      green. Five to nine is yellow, and the bank must hold more capital. Ten or more is red, and the
      model can be withdrawn.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="exhibit-wide">
    <Exhibit
      letter="H"
      title="Which zone each model sat in, day by day"
      source={`Trailing 250-day count of 99% VaR breaches, from the first full window (${baselF.dates[0]}) on, ${days.toLocaleString("en-GB")} days. Outline only: green zone. Grey: yellow zone. Red: red zone.`}
      id="exhibit-h"
    >
      <TrafficLight frame={baselF} rows={rows} label={label} />
      <TrafficLight frame={baselF} rows={rows} label={label} narrow />
      <table slot="table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Green</th><th scope="col">Yellow</th><th scope="col">Red</th><th scope="col">Most breaches</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.name}</td>
              <td>{pct(basel[r.key].green)}</td>
              <td>{pct(basel[r.key].yellow)}</td>
              <td>{pct(basel[r.key].red)}</td>
              <td>{basel[r.key].max_exceptions}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Exhibit>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The adaptive conformal model was green on {pct(basel.aci.green)} of days. The normal-distribution
      model spent {pct(basel.gaussian.yellow)} of its days in yellow, and historical simulation reached red.
      A model that stays green keeps the capital multiplier at its floor of 3.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is a catch, and it belongs in the open. Green is not free. The capital a bank must hold
      scales with the VaR itself, and the conformal limits are wider: on average they implied capital of
      {" "}{pct(basel.aci.mean_capital)} of the portfolio, against {pct(basel.fhs.mean_capital)} for filtered historical
      simulation, which was green on {pct(basel.fhs.green)} of days. On this portfolio, FHS bought nearly the
      same calibration more cheaply.
    </p>
  </div>

  <table class="data">
    <caption class="visually-hidden">Ten-day 99% VaR backtest on non-overlapping windows</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Ten-day 99% VaR</th><th scope="col" class="num">Breached</th><th scope="col" class="num">Kupiec p</th><th scope="col" class="num">Average limit</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {longRows.map((k) => (
        <tr class={k === "aci" ? "focus" : ""}>
          <th scope="row">{longNames[k]}</th>
          <td class="num">{pct(long[k].breach_rate_nonoverlap, 1)}</td>
          <td class="num">{long[k].kupiec_p.toFixed(2)}</td>
          <td class="num">{pct(long[k].mean_var, 1)}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="table-note">
    The regulatory horizon is ten days. Tested on {n10} non-overlapping ten-day windows, so a single breach
    moves the rate by {pct(1 / n10, 1)}: only historical simulation is clearly rejected. Average limit is the
    ten-day loss level, as a share of the portfolio.
  </p>

  <PractitionerNotes>
    <p>
      Exceptions are assigned to the day the loss is realized. Zones follow the Basel 1996 framework at
      250 observations (green ≤ 4, yellow 5–9 with plus-factors 0.40–0.85, red ≥ 10, multiplier 4).
      Capital is the Basel 2.5 market-risk charge, max(VaR<sub>t</sub>, k<sub>t</sub> · mean VaR over 60 days),
      on the 10-day 99% VaR. The conformal 10-day VaR is fitted on summed 10-day returns, with γ scaled
      by 1/h because consecutive targets overlap.
    </p>
  </PractitionerNotes>

  <Definitions items={[{ id: "traffic-light", term: "Traffic light", def: "Basel's backtest: breaches of 99% VaR in the last 250 days; 0–4 green, 5–9 yellow, 10+ red." }]} />
  <Pager id="regulator" />
</section>
